  "worked_solution": "Isolating the quartic-degree term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(x-5)^{4}-4 & \\ge 23 \\\\ 3(x-5)^{4} & \\ge 27 \\\\ (x-5)^{4} & \\ge 9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the fourth root of both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[4]{(x-5)^{4}} & \\ge \\sqrt[4]{9} \\\\ |x-5| & \\ge \\sqrt[4]{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\nx-5\\ge \\sqrt[4]{9}\\,\\text{or}\\,x-5\\le -\\sqrt[4]{9}\n$$\n\nWe can solve each inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-5 & \\ge \\sqrt[4]{9} &  &  & x-5 & \\le -\\sqrt[4]{9} \\\\ x & \\ge \\sqrt[4]{9}+5 &  &  & x & \\le -\\sqrt[4]{9}+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nx\\le -\\sqrt[4]{9}+5\\,\\text{or}\\,x\\ge \\sqrt[4]{9}+5.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
